.TH NETDOC\-SIM 1 "2026-09-05" "network-doctor" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
netdoc\-sim \- build throwaway virtual networks, run netdoc inside them,
 and score the diagnosis
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B netdoc\-sim
.I command
.RI [ flags ]
.RI [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B netdoc\-sim
builds a virtual network from a scenario, breaks it in a chosen way, runs
.BR netdoc (1)
inside it, and reports whether the diagnosis matched what the scenario
actually broke. It is the test harness for
.BR netdoc ,
and the way Challenge Mode is played.
.PP
Everything lives in a user namespace that owns nothing on the host: no
privilege is requested and none is needed, the host's interfaces, routes,
resolver and firewall are untouched, and the network is gone when the command
returns. Run
.B netdoc\-sim capabilities
to see what a run does on this machine and whether it can run at all.
.PP
The only backend that builds a live network is Linux namespaces.
The separate Scenario Lab models observations in memory on Linux, macOS
 and Windows, without namespaces, network access, or a separate netdoc binary.
.PP
Each command parses its own flags. Flags may be given before or after a
command's positional argument, and each accepts both the single-dash and
double-dash spelling
.RB ( \-json
and
.B \-\-json
are the same flag).
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI lab " command"
Run the deterministic, offline Scenario Lab. Commands are
.BR "list" ,
.BI "describe " NAME ,
.BI "run " NAME ,
and
.BR "run --all" .
Lab run accepts
.B \-json
for experimental machine-readable results and
.B \-trace
for simulator-only packet paths. Results distinguish injected truth,
observed evidence, production diagnosis, semantic expectations, and validation.
Exit 1 includes known reasoning failures; these are never silently waived.
.TP
.BI run " scenario"
Build the network, run the scenario's tests through
.BR netdoc ,
and print the report.
.TP
.BI campaign " scenario"
Run a seeded campaign of the scenario sequentially, from one root seed, so a
mismatch can be replayed exactly. The scenario must carry a campaign
definition; the built-in ones that do are
.BR dns\-timeout\-boundary ,
.BR flapping\-connectivity ,
and
.BR unstable\-connectivity .
.TP
.BI hunt " [base]"
Generate deterministic faults on top of a healthy base scenario, run each
generated case, and rank the cases where the diagnosis looks wrong. The
default
.B bug\-oracle
lane selects only mutations whose expected diagnosis can be checked against
independent simulator evidence. The
.B stress
lane retains useful robustness and topology mutations that do not currently
have a direct diagnosis-defect oracle. The default base is
.BR healthy\-routed\-network .
With
.B merge
in place of the base, read a complete set of shard JSON files and write one
canonical hunt JSON report in global case order.
.TP
.B triage
Hunt the fixed baselines at their fixed seeds, reproduce every candidate
finding by re-running its exact generated case, and optionally file the
survivors as GitHub issues.
.TP
.BI challenge " [id]"
Play a challenge: diagnose a hidden fault yourself in a shell inside the
broken machine, then watch
.B netdoc
take its turn on the same network. An
.I id
replays a specific challenge, and is the same thing
.B \-id
takes.
.TP
.BI validate " scenario"
Parse and check a scenario without building anything. Needs no namespaces and
no privileges.
.TP
.B scenarios
List the built-in scenario names, one per line.
.TP
.BI starters " [pack]"
List the curated starter packs, or with a pack named, that pack's challenge
ids in order, easiest first.
.TP
.B authored
List the hand-written challenges, their slugs, and the challenge ids that
play them.
.TP
.B capabilities
Report whether this host can simulate, what a run does, and what is missing
if it cannot.
.TP
.B list
List the simulations left running by
.BR "run \-keep" .
.TP
.BI inspect " id"
Show a kept simulation's nodes and the
.BR nsenter (1)
command that enters each one.
.TP
.BI cleanup " [id]"
Release a kept simulation's namespaces and files. Takes one simulation id, or
.BR \-all .
.TP
.B version
Print the build version, the same one
.B netdoc
reports for the same release.
.TP
.B help
Print usage and exit.
.PP
A
.I scenario
is a built-in name, as listed by
.BR "netdoc\-sim scenarios" ,
or a path to a YAML file.
.SH OPTIONS
Flags belong to the command they follow;
.B \-cases
is a
.B hunt
and
.B triage
flag, not a
.B run
flag. The commands not named below take no flags.
.SS lab run
.TP
.B \-all
Run every built-in Scenario Lab case.
.TP
.B \-json
Print experimental internal lab JSON.
.TP
.B \-trace
Include simulator-only packet paths.
.SS run
.TP
.B \-json
Print the machine-readable report instead of the text one.
.TP
.B \-keep
Hold the network open after the report, until interrupted, so its nodes can be
entered with
.BR nsenter (1).
The kept simulation is recorded for
.BR list ,
.B inspect
and
.BR cleanup ,
and the record is removed when this process exits. Ignored under
.BR \-dry\-run ,
which builds nothing to keep.
.TP
.BI \-netdoc " path"
The
.B netdoc
binary to run. The default is the one sitting beside
.BR netdoc\-sim ,
then the first one on
.BR $PATH .
An explicit path never falls back: a binary that cannot be executed is an
error, because running a different one would make the result a lie about which
build was measured.
.TP
.BI \-timeout " duration"
.BR netdoc 's
per-probe timeout, as a Go duration string such as
.B 2s
or
.BR 1500ms .
Must be positive. The default is
.BR 4s .
.TP
.BI \-repeat " n"
Run each test
.I n
times, to catch a diagnosis that is not stable. Must be at least 1. The
default is
.BR 1 .
.TP
.B \-dry\-run
Print every privileged command the run would make, and stop. Creates no
namespaces, so it works on a host that cannot simulate at all.
.TP
.B \-v
Log each privileged command as it runs.
.SS campaign
.TP
.BI \-runs " n"
Override the campaign's own bounded run count. Between 1 and 1000 when
supplied; the default,
.BR 0 ,
leaves the campaign's definition in charge.
.TP
.BI \-seed " int64"
Root seed every iteration is derived from. Generated and printed when omitted,
so a run can always be replayed by passing back the seed it reported.
.TP
.BI \-iteration " n"
Run exactly one independently derived iteration, between 0 and 999999.
Combined with
.BR \-runs ,
that one iteration is repeated
.I n
times.
.TP
.B \-fail\-fast
Stop after the first mismatch or simulator error instead of finishing the
campaign.
.TP
.B \-json
Print the machine-readable aggregate report.
.TP
.BI \-netdoc " path"
The
.B netdoc
binary to run; see
.B \-netdoc
under
.BR run .
.TP
.BI \-timeout " duration"
.BR netdoc 's
per-probe timeout. Must be positive. The default is
.BR 4s .
.TP
.B \-v
Log each privileged command as it runs.
.SS hunt
.TP
.BI \-cases " n"
Logical unique cases before shard filtering, between 1 and 500. The default is
.BR 50 .
.TP
.BI \-seed " int64"
Hunt seed. Generated and printed when omitted.
.TP
.BI \-case " n"
Generate and run exactly one independently derived case, between 0 and 999999.
This is how a reported finding is reproduced: the same seed, case number,
fault ceiling, generator version, and lane regenerate the same network and
fault.
.TP
.BI \-shard " i/N"
Run zero-based shard
.I i
of
.I N
over the existing global case sequence. An accepted global case belongs to
shard
.I i
when its case number modulo
.I N
equals
.IR i .
Cannot be combined with
.BR \-case .
.TP
.BI \-max\-faults " n"
Maximum mutations applied to one case, between 1 and 3. The default is
.BR 2 .
.TP
.BI \-generator\-version " version"
Select the generator used to materialize Hunt cases. Supported versions are
.BR v3 ,
.BR v4 ,
.BR v5 ,
.BR v6 ,
and
.BR v7 .
The default is the current generator,
.BR v7 .
Historical reproduction commands record and pass this value explicitly.
.TP
.BI \-lane " name"
Select
.B bug\-oracle
or
.BR stress .
Bug-oracle mutations have a direct analyzer contract comparing independent
simulator evidence with Network Doctor's diagnosis. Stress mutations remain
useful for robustness, unusual topology, interaction, parser, and execution
exploration, but currently lack that direct diagnosis-defect contract. The
default for the current generator is
.BR bug\-oracle .
Generators v3 through v5 predate the split and use
.B all
for exact historical replay.
.TP
.B \-fail\-fast
Stop after the first case with a reportable finding.
.TP
.B \-dry\-run
Print the generated manifests without creating any namespaces, so the
generator can be inspected on a host that cannot simulate.
.TP
.B \-json
Print the machine-readable hunt report.
.TP
.BI \-netdoc " path"
The
.B netdoc
binary to run; see
.B \-netdoc
under
.BR run .
.TP
.BI \-timeout " duration"
.BR netdoc 's
per-probe timeout. Must be positive. The default is
.BR 4s .
.TP
.B \-v
Log each privileged command as it runs.
.PP
The base scenario is a positional argument, and has to be one of the healthy
bases the generator knows how to break:
.BR dual\-stack\-healthy ,
.BR healthy ,
.BR healthy\-routed\-network ,
.BR socks5h\-remote\-dns\-succeeds ,
.BR tls\-valid ,
.BR two\-path\-healthy ,
.BR two\-path\-ipv6\-healthy ,
.BR two\-router\-healthy .
.SS triage
.TP
.BI \-scenarios " list"
Comma-separated baselines to hunt. The default is every baseline:
.BR healthy ,
.BR healthy\-routed\-network ,
.BR dual\-stack\-healthy ,
.BR tls\-valid ,
.BR socks5h\-remote\-dns\-succeeds .
Each carries a fixed seed for stable regression coverage. Automated
exploration may override all selected baselines with one recorded seed.
.TP
.BI \-cases " n"
Unique generated cases per baseline, between 1 and 500. The default is
.BR 20 .
.TP
.BI \-hunt\-results " directory"
Use one canonical merged hunt JSON report per selected baseline from this
directory instead of running the full hunts again. Exact finding cases are
still re-run in fresh namespaces before triage can file them.
.TP
.BI \-seed " int64"
Override the fixed seed of every selected baseline. Normally left alone: the
fixed seeds provide the default regression inputs.
.TP
.BI \-max\-faults " n"
Maximum mutations per case, between 1 and 3. The default is
.BR 2 .
.TP
.BI \-lane " name"
Select the Hunt lane to run or to match in
.BR \-hunt\-results .
The default is
.BR bug\-oracle .
Use
.B all
only when triaging reports from a historical generator.
.TP
.BI \-min\-severity " level"
Lowest finding severity worth reproducing and filing, one of
.BR critical ,
.BR high ,
.BR medium ,
.BR low ,
.BR info .
The default is
.BR medium :
reproducing a case costs a whole simulated run, so the floor is applied before
the work rather than after it.
.TP
.B \-create
File each reproducible finding as a GitHub issue, through the
.BR gh (1)
CLI. Open issues are searched for the finding's fingerprint first, so a
finding that is already tracked is recorded rather than filed twice. Without
this flag nothing leaves the machine.
.TP
.BI \-context " text"
Debugging context recorded in the issue body, such as which job produced the
run.
.TP
.BI \-revision " sha"
Commit to record in the issue body. The default is the VCS revision stamped
into this binary at build time.
.TP
.B \-json
Print the machine-readable triage report.
.TP
.BI \-netdoc " path"
The
.B netdoc
binary to run; see
.B \-netdoc
under
.BR run .
.TP
.BI \-timeout " duration"
.BR netdoc 's
per-probe timeout. Must be positive. The default is
.BR 4s .
.TP
.B \-v
Log each privileged command as it runs.
.SS challenge
.TP
.BI \-id " ID"
Replay a specific challenge instead of drawing one, by the id printed with
every challenge. The same id is accepted as a positional argument.
.TP
.BI \-daily "[=YYYY-MM-DD]"
Play the challenge for today, or for that UTC date. A daily is the same
challenge for everybody who asks for that date, so it cannot be combined with
.BR \-id ,
.BR \-difficulty ,
.BR \-starter ,
or
.BR \-authored .
The result ends with a spoiler\-free share block naming no fault, and a daily
also asks the terminal to copy that block to the clipboard once the matchup is
decided. That is best effort: a terminal that does not support the request, or
output that is not going to one, changes neither the result nor the exit
status, and the block is printed either way.
.TP
.BI \-starter " pack"
Draw from a curated starter pack, one of
.BR fundamentals ,
.BR dns ,
.BR service ,
.BR tls ,
.BR paths ,
.BR routing .
.B netdoc\-sim starters
describes them, and
.B netdoc\-sim starters
.I pack
lists one pack's challenge ids in order.
.TP
.BI \-authored " slug"
Play a hand-written challenge by slug, as listed by
.BR "netdoc\-sim authored" :
.BR resolver\-refuses ,
.BR resolver\-goes\-quiet ,
.BR refused\-vs\-blocked\-refused ,
.BR refused\-vs\-blocked\-blocked ,
.BR reset\-after\-accept ,
.BR certificate\-expired ,
.BR certificate\-wrong\-name ,
.BR no\-default\-route ,
.BR wrong\-default\-route ,
.BR missing\-subnet\-route .
.TP
.BI \-difficulty " level"
Draw an
.BR easy ,
.B medium
or
.B hard
challenge. Difficulty is a property of the case an id resolves to, so this
only applies to a drawn challenge, never to one already named.
.TP
.BI \-answer " name"
Submit this diagnosis without opening a shell, for scripting a run rather than
playing it. One of
.BR healthy ,
.BR dns_failure ,
.BR no_default_route ,
.BR wrong_default_route ,
.BR missing_subnet_route ,
.BR preferred_route_failure ,
.BR ipv4_failure ,
.BR ipv6_failure ,
.BR tcp_port_blocked ,
.BR connection_refused ,
.BR connection_reset ,
.BR tls_certificate ,
.BR tls_hostname_mismatch ,
.BR http_service ,
.BR proxy_failure ,
.BR quic_udp_blocked ,
.BR packet_loss .
.TP
.B \-give\-up
Skip straight to the answer, scoring the round as a give-up.
.TP
.B \-json
Print the machine-readable result. It runs without a terminal, so it needs
.B \-answer
or
.BR \-give\-up .
.TP
.BI \-netdoc " path"
The
.B netdoc
binary to run; see
.B \-netdoc
under
.BR run .
Which build it is becomes part of the recorded result.
.TP
.BI \-timeout " duration"
.BR netdoc 's
per-probe timeout. Must be positive. The default is
.BR 4s .
.TP
.B \-v
Log each privileged command as it runs.
.SS cleanup
.TP
.B \-all
Release every kept simulation. Takes no simulation id.
.SH PLAYING A CHALLENGE
A challenge session is one foreground command, start to finish: the network
only exists while the process holding its namespaces does.
.PP
The session briefs you, then opens a shell in the broken machine. Type
.B exit
there when you are ready, and a menu asks for your diagnosis. Pick one by
number or by name;
.B b
rereads the briefing,
.B s
returns to the shell, and
.B q
gives up. Your solve time is the shell and the menu only \(em not the
simulator's setup, and not
.BR netdoc 's
own run, which happens after your answer is locked in.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The diagnosis matched. For
.BR hunt ,
no reportable finding. For
.BR challenge ,
your diagnosis was correct. For
.BR triage ,
nothing reproducible, or every reproducible finding is now tracked by an
issue.
.TP
.B 1
The simulation ran and the diagnosis did not match. For
.BR hunt ,
there are findings. For
.BR challenge ,
your diagnosis was wrong or you gave up. For
.BR triage ,
there are reproducible findings nobody recorded.
.TP
.B 2
Bad arguments, or a scenario the parser rejected.
.TP
.B 3
The simulation could not run: an unsupported host, a
.B netdoc
that could not be found or executed, or a cancelled run. For
.BR triage ,
also a failed
.B gh
call.
.SH EXAMPLES
Check whether this host can simulate at all:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
netdoc\-sim capabilities
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run one scenario and read the report:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
netdoc\-sim run broken\-dns
.EE
.RE
.PP
See what a run would do, without creating anything:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
netdoc\-sim run broken\-dns \-\-dry\-run
.EE
.RE
.PP
Keep the network up afterwards and walk around inside it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
netdoc\-sim run broken\-dns \-\-keep
netdoc\-sim list
netdoc\-sim inspect <id>
netdoc\-sim cleanup <id>
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run a seeded campaign, then replay the iteration that failed:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
netdoc\-sim campaign flapping\-connectivity
netdoc\-sim campaign flapping\-connectivity \-\-seed 1234 \-\-iteration 7
.EE
.RE
.PP
Hunt for diagnoses that look wrong, then reproduce one exactly:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
netdoc\-sim hunt \-\-lane bug\-oracle \-\-cases 100 \-\-json > hunt.json
netdoc\-sim hunt \-\-lane stress \-\-cases 20 \-\-json > stress.json
netdoc\-sim hunt healthy\-routed\-network \-\-lane all \-\-seed 1234 \-\-case 42 \-\-max\-faults 2 \-\-generator\-version v3
netdoc\-sim hunt \-\-lane bug\-oracle \-\-seed 1234 \-\-cases 60 \-\-shard 0/4 \-\-json > shard0.json
netdoc\-sim hunt merge shard3.json shard2.json shard1.json shard0.json > hunt.json
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run the nightly triage without filing anything:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
netdoc\-sim triage \-\-cases 20 \-\-json
.EE
.RE
.PP
Play a challenge, or today's:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
netdoc\-sim challenge
netdoc\-sim challenge \-\-daily
netdoc\-sim challenge \-\-starter fundamentals
netdoc\-sim challenge V4\-8F42C1
.EE
.RE
.PP
Score a challenge from a script, with no terminal:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
netdoc\-sim challenge \-\-id V4\-8F42C1 \-\-answer connection_refused \-\-json
.EE
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/netdoc\-sim/
One JSON record per simulation kept alive by
.BR "run \-keep" ,
which is what
.BR list ,
.B inspect
and
.B cleanup
read. Without
.B $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
this is
.I netdoc\-sim\-$UID
under the temporary directory instead. A record never outlives the namespaces
it describes; a stale one is swept by
.BR cleanup .
.TP
.I $TMPDIR/netdoc\-sim\-<id>
The scratch directory belonging to one simulation, removed with it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR netdoc (1),
.BR nsenter (1),
.BR unshare (1),
.BR gh (1)
.PP
